Droppen server — macOS (@ARCH@)
===============================

This is the Droppen server, for running Droppen in the background or on a
device with no screen. For everyday use, the Droppen desktop App is the
recommended way: https://github.com/getdroppen/droppen/releases/latest

Droppen was called remote-hand. The server is now `droppen`; the binary its
supervisor runs, `<data>/bin/remote-hand-server`, and its data directory keep
the old name, so an existing install carries on unchanged.

Droppen is licensed under AGPL-3.0-only (see LICENSE); THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES lists
the third-party software in the server and its licences.
Copyright (C) 2026 Kilerd

@CONTENTS@
Install it as `remote-hand-server` — the supervisor looks for exactly
`<data>/bin/remote-hand-server` — with `droppen` beside it, the command you
type:

    mkdir -p ~/.local/share/remote-hand/bin
    mv remote-hand-server ~/.local/share/remote-hand/bin/
    chmod +x ~/.local/share/remote-hand/bin/remote-hand-server
    ln -sf remote-hand-server ~/.local/share/remote-hand/bin/droppen

If you downloaded the tarball with a browser, macOS attached a quarantine flag
and Gatekeeper will refuse to run the binary. Clear it (a `curl` download has
no such flag):

    xattr -d com.apple.quarantine ~/.local/share/remote-hand/bin/remote-hand-server

Run it under its supervisor, which is what makes self-update and
restart-survival work:

    ~/.local/share/remote-hand/bin/remote-hand-server up -- serve

Then open http://127.0.0.1:8700.

This server binds 127.0.0.1 by default and asks every browser who it is.
Until it has an account it shows a setup page:
`~/.local/share/remote-hand/bin/droppen open` on this device opens it signed in
as the device's owner, and from anywhere else the first account takes the
one-time code the server keeps (`droppen setup-code`). Create the account with
`~/.local/share/remote-hand/bin/droppen user add <name>` (or on the page)
before exposing it beyond localhost; from then on every request needs a login.
Access to this server includes files and interactive shells. For remote access,
use an authenticating reverse proxy, an SSH tunnel, or Tailscale.

The one-line installer does all of the above, creates the first account,
and starts the server at login with launchd:

    curl -fsSL https://droppen.app/install.sh | sh

Everything else, including how to uninstall:
https://github.com/getdroppen/droppen#readme
